Since $g(x)=\dfrac18x^3+\dfrac12x-\dfrac14$, we know that $g'(x)=\dfrac38x^2-\dfrac12$.

$\begin{align}
g'(2)&=\dfrac38(2)^2+\dfrac12
\\\\
&=2
\end{align}$

Now let's put everything together:

$\begin{align}
h'\left(\dfrac74\right)&=\dfrac{1}{g'\Bigl(h\left(\dfrac74\right)\Bigr)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1}{g'(2)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1}{2}
\end{align}$
